I've identified **serious issues** that should block this merge. Here's my analysis:

### 🔴 **Critical: Missing Authentication**
The new `exportOrdersHandler` lacks the `requireUser(req)` check that all other handlers use. This exposes **all orders from all users** to unauthenticated requests — a data breach.

```javascript
// ❌ Current - Anyone can call this
export function exportOrdersHandler(req) {
  const rows = orders.map((order) => `${order.id},${order.userId},${order.productId},${order.quantity},${order.note}`);
  ...
}

// ✅ Should be
export function exportOrdersHandler(req) {
  const denied = requireUser(req);
  if (denied) return denied;
  // ... rest of handler
}
```

### 🔴 **Critical: Missing Data Filtering**
The handler exports ALL orders in the system. Per your comment and existing patterns (see `listOrdersHandler`), it should only export the signed-in user's orders:

```javascript
// ✅ Should filter to user
const mine = orders.filter((order) => order.userId === req.user.id);
const rows = mine.map((order) => `${order.id},${order.userId},${order.productId},${order.quantity},${order.note}`);
```

### 🟡 **Minor: Missing CSV Escaping**
If `note` or other fields contain commas or newlines, the CSV will be malformed. Consider escaping values or using a proper CSV library.

